Task: Resilience.middleware composition (ADR 0010), plus a correction to Retry discovered while writing this task's own test.

Resilience.middleware[F, A](config)(logger)(meter): Guard[F] composes
Retry (outer) around CircuitBreaker (inner), mirroring purerest.resilience.Resilience's
own documented ordering and reasoning exactly.

Correction folded into this commit (not a separate task - discovered via
TDD while writing ResilienceSuite, before Phase 1's checkpoint): the
original Retry.middleware (committed 57bec6f) retried every raised error
unconditionally. Composed naively as retryGuard(breakerGuard(fa)), that
would retry a CircuitBreakerOpen rejection too, directly contradicting
this track's own plan.md ('a breaker-open rejection isn't itself
retried'). Fixed by adding an optional nonRetriable: Throwable => Boolean
parameter to Retry.middleware (default _ => false, preserving existing
retry-everything behavior and all of RetrySuite's prior assertions
unchanged), added to the existing meter parameter list rather than a new
curried list (Scala requires every parameter in a list to be supplied or
defaulted together - appending to an existing list keeps every current
3-arg call site source-compatible). Resilience.middleware passes
nonRetriable = _ == CircuitBreakerOpen. Uses cats-retry's
ResultHandler.retryOnSomeErrors (not retryOnAllErrors) - confirmed its
real parameter name is , not , by decompiling the
resolved cats-retry_3-4.0.0 jar directly (javap) rather than guessing.

Also caught and fixed a flawed first draft of ResilienceSuite's own
'breaker opens' test before this commit: naively assumed each top-level
guard(fa) call maps to exactly one circuit-breaker failure event, but
since Retry retries by re-running the ENTIRE breaker-wrapped computation
(not just the inner fa), each individual retry attempt hits the breaker
separately too - so a single top-level call with maxRetries=2 can itself
trip the breaker after only 2 of its own 3 attempts. Rewrote the
'opens after threshold' test with maxRetries=0 to isolate that scenario
cleanly, and the 'not retried' test to assert on the breaker's own
purekafka.circuit_breaker.calls_rejected metric (1 rejection, not 2) -
the fa-call count alone can't distinguish 'retried-and-rejected-again'
from 'failed-fast', since fa never runs on a rejected attempt either way.

Verified: sbt pureKafka/test (scoped to this module) - 13 passed, 0
failed, 0 errors (CircuitBreakerSuite 3, ResilienceSuite 3, RetrySuite
7 - RetrySuite grew by one test covering nonRetriable directly, in
addition to the 6 already covered by 57bec6f).
